+#### Differentiable Radiance Derivative Computation
+A method to compute radiance derivatives with respect to density analytically, avoiding gradient tracking and redundant operations inherent in automatic differentiation (AD) [S4, unofficial]. It derives an analytical form of the radiance transfer equation [S4, unofficial]. Derive discrete forms of transmittance $\tau$ and single-scattering radiance $L_{ss}$ from their continuous integral forms to facilitate computer program implementation [S4, unofficial]. Unlike automatic differentiation (AD) which propagates gradients through long computational graphs carrying inherent overhead, this method directly computes derivatives analytically, avoiding gradient tracking and redundant operations [S4, unofficial].
+
+## Operating characteristics
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the implementation. Analytic methods can be $O(1)$ per pixel for simple geometries but require precomputation of shadow/illumination volumes [S1]. Deterministic voxel-based methods are $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels [S1]. Stochastic methods (NEE) are $O(L \cdot S)$ where $L$ is the number of lights and $S$ is the number of samples per light [S5566].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Voxel-based methods require $O(V)$ memory for the voxel grid, where $V$ is the number of voxels [S1]. Analytic methods require memory for shadow volumes [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Analytical methods can achieve real-time frame rates for simple scenes [S3]. Stochastic methods are slower but more general [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Computationally efficient compared to multiple scattering; suitable for real-time applications in optically thin media [S1][S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Fails for dense, high-albedo media (e.g., clouds, smoke) [S1][S2]; ignores self-illumination and glow [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Optically thin media (fog, haze, smoke with low density); real-time applications where performance is critical and visual fidelity for dense media is not required [S1][S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Dense clouds, thick smoke, or any medium with high albedo ($\Omega > 0.3$) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of single scattering was first introduced by Kajiya [KH84] as the first model allowing for scattering rendering, using a two-pass method [S1, unofficial]. Max [Max86] extended Blinn's layer-based model to arbitrary shapes using analytical functions, and later [Max94] added anisotropic light dispersion [S1, unofficial]. Blinn's model itself is limited to layer-shaped media [S1, unofficial]. Modern implementations often use ray marching or Monte Carlo integration to solve the single-scattering integral for heterogeneous media [5566-single-scattering.md, 2293-volume-single-scattering-monte-carlo-with-point-light.md].
+Single scattering approximations were among the first methods developed for rendering participating media. Blinn [11] was the first researcher concerned with the visualization of participating media, solving the integral transport equation analytically for cloud layers with constant density and light sources/viewers at infinity [S1]. Willis [106] focused on flight simulators, assuming isotropic emission from media points [S1]. Kajiya and Von Herzen [41] extended Blinn's model for ray tracing using a voxel model [S1]. Max [54] presented a scan-line based method using shadow volumes [S1]. Nishita et al. [62] used ray casting with shadow and illumination volumes [S1]. These methods are often strongly related to specific media or problems, making them difficult to adapt to general situations [S1].
 
